this video originally aired as part of  teching one-on-ones charity stream to  benefit the Children's Tumor Foundation  please check the link in the description  to learn more and be sure to show  teching 101 son love he is genuinely one  of the coolest and nicest people on  YouTube  [Music]  what is up everybody its Anna Mack here  for anime uproar and in this video we  will be talking about the one thing that  we can learn from almost all shown and  protagonists and this one thing is an  absolutely essential lesson to not only  learn but also apply in our own daily  lives shown an anime and manga may be  primarily geared towards a male teenager  audience but this particular lesson  never gets old it's not gender specific  and it is as useful for a teenager as it  is for an octogenarian we've all noticed  it before even if we haven't actually  given it much thought and in many ways  this has become a cliche with Shonen  protagonists but the fact is that almost  every shown in MC has a clear  overarching goal or purpose Luffy wants  to become the Pirate King Ashe wants to  become a Pokemon master  Naruto wants to become the Hokage ah  stuff from black clover wants to become  the wizard King Dec who wants to become  the greatest hero  Goku wants to get stronger and keep  fighting stronger opponents and so on in  sports anime the protagonist usually  wants to be the best he can be or for  his team to do as well as possible and  the MC and food Wars wants to become the  sexiest chef or whatever I still have to  watch that one so I'm just assuming here  sometimes the protagonists overall goal  isn't necessarily an affirmative one for  instance Eren from attack on Titan wants  to kill all the Titans and write the  horrible injustice that he had to endure  as a child when he lost his home and his  mother to the Titans now I know that  there are people out there who can't  really relate to Eren but as someone who  lived through the Yugoslav civil wars as  a child and was a refugee during the war  I understand how Aaron feels thankfully  I didn't lose any members of my  immediate family during the war but my  family did lose everything we owned  including our home and we had to flee  and start over from scratch other  protagonists like Mellie Otis from the  seven deadly sins have an overarching  goal that isn't revealed until later and  I won't mention what it is right now so  as to avoid spoilers but the fact is  that the vast majority of Shonen  protagonists have some sort of clear  goal some purpose that drives their act  even though in most stories the journey  is what really matters not necessarily  the destination so for example even if  Luffy never actually becomes the Pirate  King fans of one piece will still love  the series but the MCS goal does matter  because it drives his actions and  informs his decisions  if Luffy's goal was to be a small town  fisherman or if deck whose goal was to  be an accountant the stories of one  piece and boku no hero would be very  different so we know that the purpose of  a shounen protagonists matters for the  story but what exactly does that have to  do with the real world as cliche as it  sounds to want to be the best of this or  the greatest that it is actually  extremely important for human beings to  have a clear purpose to their lives from  the philosophers of ancient Greece like  Plato and Aristotle and Romans like epic  titus and marcus aurelius to modern day  thinkers and gurus of self-development  like Dale Carnegie and Tony Robbins wise  people have been saying the same thing  for thousands of years humans need to  have a purpose in their lives in order  to feel happy and fulfilled positive  goals and a clear purpose gives our  lives meaning it inspires and motivates  us to work harder and reach greater  heights Heights that we could never have  even imagined if we didn't have a goal  that drives us since you and me are not  anime protagonists we don't have to be  the next master wizard pirate king of  heroes but we do all need a positive  goal a passion and a purpose that drives  us and inspires us this could be  something as simple as providing for  your family or helping others by  volunteering or working as a nurse or it  could be something ambitious like  becoming the president ending world  hunger by inventing a new way to farm  food or finding the cure for terrible  diseases in the modern age many people  have lost sight of how important it is  to have a purpose and many of us drift  through life without knowing what  exactly we want out of it we don't know  what to study we don't know what type of  career we want and so many of us end up  studying and doing what other people  such as our parents want us to do  instead of what we want to do our entire  lives we are told that what matters most  is going to  cool so that we can find a good job so  that we can make lots of money and so on  but money in itself is not a goal it's  not a purpose sure we need money to  survive but money is not the end goal if  your primary goal in life is just to  make as much money as possible you can  never be happy or satisfied there is  always more money out there to be made  there is always someone richer than you  someone with an advantage that you don't  have that gives them the upper hand over  you and so on trying to make as much  money as possible just means that you  will work yourself too hard because  every moment that you are resting or  doing something else you will feel  guilty that you're not out there making  more money at that point money which can  be a blessing becomes a curse and an  addiction and eventually being obsessed  with making more money will have a  negative impact on our relationships and  on our physical health in the past most  people found their life's purpose in  religion religious teachings told us how  to live our lives if we want to be  rewarded in the afterlife  and the meaning of life was defined by  the values of our religion and while  many people are still religious today in  the modern world in which the focus is  on science and physical reality rather  than belief and metaphysics there are a  lot more people who no longer embrace  religion as their primary purpose in  life there are certainly many negatives  to embracing any doctrine religious or  otherwise without questioning or  thinking for yourself so the fact that  we no longer live in a world that is  controlled by strict religious  fundamentalism  is a good thing however there are also  negative consequences to having no  purpose at all and with the movement of  modern society away from relying  exclusively on religion for purpose and  meaning there is a danger that we will  be left without any purpose at all  wandering meaninglessly through life not  knowing who we want to be what we want  to do and what if anything we want to  achieve in Western liberal democratic  societies in particular there is a heavy  emphasis on individualism and everyone  doing whatever they want as long as they  don't violate the rights of others but  while this may work for people who are  decisive and know exactly what they want  in life there are many others who don't  know what they want to do in life or who  they want to be and this can create  serious problems for them  if you are pressured to go to school and  study and then find a job that makes you  lots of money but you have no idea what  you want to do and no guidance other  than you need to get a job and make  money chances are that you will feel  pressured to do something that you don't  want to do and you won't be happy even  if you do get a good job and make lots  of money you may be doing something that  you hate and if you can't cope with the  pressure to find that high paying job  you may withdraw from society altogether  becoming a permanent NEET or even a  hikikomori Japan obviously has an issue  with this type of strict social pressure  having a negative impact on some  segments of society but this pressure is  present in other nations as well and  this is where the Shonan protagonists  come in each and every one of us  regardless of what other people are  telling us or expecting from us needs to  stop and think what do we really want to  do in life what makes us happy what is  our purpose in this sense Shonen  protagonists are great role models  because they often have that clear  purpose sometimes it is an  unconventional purpose a purpose that  breaks the mold of society like for  example deque who wanting to be the  strongest hero even though he is quark  lists or hasta wanting to be the wizard  king even though he has no magic if  these guys were just listening to  everyone around them and following the  norms of their society they would have  given up on their dreams and gotten  regular jobs that might not make them  happy but would make them enough money  to lead a normal life and of course  eventually these two protagonists get  lucky thanks to plot convenience and dec  who inherits a quark that makes him  strong  while asta gets the ability to use ante  magic to nullify the magic of others but  while that is definitely plot  convenience the fact is that if deku and  asta had given up on their dreams after  realizing that they had no natural  abilities they never would have been in  the position to get lucky in the first  place  back when my brother goes in and I  started working on our YouTube channel  together I was in school studying a  completely unrelated field and I had no  knowledge of video editing or the  business aspect of YouTube if I just  stuck with what I knew and didn't go out  on a limb to try something new I never  would have become an anime youtuber in  the first place I certainly wouldn't  have become one if I'd listened to what  others thought and to the social  pressures of  around me because my parents and almost  everyone else I knew told me to focus on  getting a quote real job and stop  wasting time on YouTube of course at  some point we got lucky and the videos  we put out on our channel caught  traction and not everyone gets to that  point but if I had listened to the  social pressures and if I hadn't taken a  shot in the dark with YouTube I never  would have gotten to the point where I  could get lucky in the first place  just like dec who if he had given up on  his dream of being a hero when he found  out that he was quicklist never would  have had the opportunity to inherit one  for all and this is one of the big life  lessons that we can learn from shown and  protagonists think about what you would  want to do in life think about what you  consider to be fun and meaningful and  what would make you happy identify your  goal identify your purpose and then  pursue it pursue it even if it's an  unconventional goal even if the odds are  stacked against you and most people are  telling you to give it up if it's  something you love then the journey will  be fun  even without the destination and who  knows you might get lucky and find some  kind of powerup on the way that changes  your entire situation like Deku  inheriting one for all or a YouTube  channel getting a hit video that  suddenly helps the channel grow  exponentially anyway thank you all so  much for watching and feel free to give  me your feedback on this video in the  comments or on social media just hit me  up on Twitter and Instagram at anime  uproar I want to give a big thank you to  our anime poor patrons for supporting  this type of content including urbano  Tovar the one and only Gilgamesh and  originally if you'd like to support more  analytical and motivational content on  this channel consider becoming a patron  link in the description if you guys  enjoyed this type of video you can leave  a like to let me know I'll be happy to  make more in the future and until next  time see ya space cowboys  [Music]  you
 

